#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179416 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "ccfb8833-f270-5855-8c49-fb3b6b6662d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18776" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $5^{x+2}=9^{x-2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{x + 2} &= 9^{x - 2} \\\\\n\\ln (5^{x + 2}) &= \\ln (9^{x - 2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)\\ln(5) = (x-2)\\ln(9)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)\\ln (5) &= (x - 2)\\ln (9) \\\\\nx\\ln (5) + 2\\ln (5) &= x\\ln (9) - 2\\ln (9) \\\\\nx\\ln (5) - x\\ln (9) &= -2\\ln (9) - 2\\ln (5) \\\\\nx\\ln (9) - x\\ln (5) &= 2\\ln (9) + 2\\ln (5) \\\\\nx(\\ln (9) - \\ln (5)) &= 2(\\ln (5) + \\ln (9)) \\\\\nx &= (2(\\ln (5) + \\ln (9)))/(\\ln (9) - \\ln (5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "453d4ec3-98f5-5db1-a046-a6b15c13049d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4097" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of $\\log_2$, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $2^{x+1}=3^x$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ on both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{x + 1} &= 3^{x} \\\\\n\\log_{2} (2^{x + 1}) &= \\log_{2} (3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_b \\left(b^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\log_{2} (3) \\\\\nx\\log_{2} (3) - x &= 1 \\\\\nx(\\log_{2} (3) - 1) &= 1 \\\\\nx &= (1)/(\\log_{2} (3) - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "fbb90aa1-5adf-5c6d-8499-68eea953cc42" :question/math-academy-id "e-4098" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $e^{x+1}=2^x\\cdot 5$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x + 1} &= 2^{x} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{x + 1}) &= \\ln (2^{x} \\cdot 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(e^a \\right) = a\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 =\\ln \\left(2^{x}\\cdot 5\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\ln (2^{x}) + \\ln (5) \\\\\nx + 1 &= x\\ln (2) + \\ln (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\ln (2) + \\ln (5) \\\\\nx - x\\ln (2) &= \\ln (5) - 1 \\\\\nx(1 - \\ln (2)) &= \\ln (5) - 1 \\\\\nx &= (\\ln (5) - 1)/(1 - \\ln (2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0407ab3-02ee-5e73-8d86-bbe7c19dd12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251254} {:answer/id #uuid "4595fedc-25f7-57f2-a34c-c7c330f1d7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251255} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb77a6c-56e4-503c-a9ab-a187512f6a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251256} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3d6897-5649-5960-b6ae-2b6a01ec1396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251257} {:answer/id #uuid "a703d74c-6bd8-5632-9e33-bff6ac948185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0407ab3-02ee-5e73-8d86-bbe7c19dd12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251254} :answer-field/id #uuid "c979e788-3ee6-5ed6-84ca-e4c387d73cb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251253}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dcbfd20b-f656-5999-870b-b45683d40339" :question/math-academy-id "q-100471" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${3}^{x+5}={7}^{6-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+5} & ={7}^{6-2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{6-2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d94a2bf-774e-5bec-9091-2d8a83f64dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251261} {:answer/id #uuid "b709621b-4842-5e91-bdfb-6409a2a64690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251262} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6ee712-c984-505d-808e-d3afdc8368f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251263} {:answer/id #uuid "ea307155-4fd8-54bd-8c6f-5c6e48687f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251264} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4a6c1a-5b52-51fb-9858-5a4f8e73cbf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d94a2bf-774e-5bec-9091-2d8a83f64dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251261} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d244604-b1e0-50ce-a8ef-ee3e034123f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251260}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "961e98e7-83f1-521d-b29d-f7d45b2c75f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-100472" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${2}^{x+3}={9}^{8-5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+3} & ={9}^{8-5x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{8-5x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(8-5x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(8-5x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf8e4434-1802-5c8e-be31-df2b56fba857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251268} {:answer/id #uuid "2c37f8d7-1d47-5d1d-a8cc-1e51da99f877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251269} {:answer/id #uuid "55f0cc8f-4beb-5dc5-81ef-3d5b824e306e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251270} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfc6fac-cdab-5eb8-85fe-89d2a89c6a26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251271} {:answer/id #uuid "c539139d-2c41-58be-b2fc-7910e236a7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c539139d-2c41-58be-b2fc-7910e236a7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251272} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2283c37-fc3d-5c24-b59a-65e299a2cd14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251267}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "386df23c-8f42-53d4-b137-e274cd1470c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-100482" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${4}^{x+7}={5}^{8-4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x+7} & ={5}^{8-4x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x+7}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{8-4x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =(8-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =(8-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fcaebca-cab8-533b-8890-7b9e1f628c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{1+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251275} {:answer/id #uuid "0c792d3e-7096-5d22-b213-f53e91a6409d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251276} {:answer/id #uuid "988dc7fb-e0a5-5faa-b378-00b15774a54d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251277} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1e9b47-3151-5f52-a03d-a411a0b05931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251278} {:answer/id #uuid "203dd4eb-be6e-59cd-add0-497d7e174b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fcaebca-cab8-533b-8890-7b9e1f628c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{1+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251275} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e6162b3-6a3d-59f3-87d1-504aa5c92d11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251274}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47457fc5-4715-5250-8afb-4edbb48c0413" :question/math-academy-id "q-110545" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{y}={8}^{1-2y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & ={8}^{1-2y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{1-2y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{1-2y})\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\ny=(1-2y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(1-2y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ y(1+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{1+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87f1ee2-1ef7-5b1c-b22c-96427b594c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.791" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b87f1ee2-1ef7-5b1c-b22c-96427b594c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.791" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251282} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1bdacb-07a0-5d62-a827-f3d945513621" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251281}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fbe8518-3b94-57e7-a090-a9853e4fce38" :question/math-academy-id "q-242899" :question/problem "Given that ${8}^{1-x}={3}^{x/2},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{8}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{1-x} & ={3}^{x/2} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡({8}^{1-x}) & ={log}_{8}⁡({3}^{x/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n1-x={log}_{8}⁡({3}^{x/2})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n1-x=\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-x & =\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3 \\\\ 1 & =x+\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3 \\\\ x+\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3 & =1 \\\\ 2x+x{log}_{8}⁡3 & =2 \\\\ x(2+{log}_{8}⁡3) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{2+{log}_{8}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{8}⁡(3)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}≈0.528\\,321.\n$$\n\nFinally,  we can approximate $x$ as \n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2}{2+0.528\\,321}≈0.791,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "715a26cb-ec55-5a22-ba50-cd6081ec7ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\ln(6)}{3-2\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251285} {:answer/id #uuid "05cb3666-b42d-5153-a1bc-adb70cc644b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\ln(6)}{3+2\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "715a26cb-ec55-5a22-ba50-cd6081ec7ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\ln(6)}{3-2\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251285} :answer-field/id #uuid "938a59ad-4ed3-5929-8558-874207b631aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251284}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c85f3bb-5b0c-5344-bb83-aedc5a84595c" :question/math-academy-id "q-242903" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{3x-1}={6}^{2x+1}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x-1} & ={6}^{2x+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n3x-1=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x(3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}{3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2472b62e-532d-532c-bdd9-8bf800d61936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1+3\\ln(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2472b62e-532d-532c-bdd9-8bf800d61936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1+3\\ln(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251289} :answer-field/id #uuid "29387e85-e297-562f-a5f3-bddfca71e012" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251288}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48d8d194-845b-5ebe-96aa-f1c5bae21aa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-242906" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-2}={10}^{-3x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-2} & ={10}^{-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({10}^{-3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({10}^{-3x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) & =2 \\\\ x(1+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4d1e211-be1e-51cd-9f19-6da94007bb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\ln(10)}{\\ln(10)-2\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4d1e211-be1e-51cd-9f19-6da94007bb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\ln(10)}{\\ln(10)-2\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251292} :answer-field/id #uuid "8920f265-8ec3-596e-9dfb-4e308a1fba6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251291}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f0395a0-8645-5c1d-b942-d51d175720a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-242918" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${8}^{2x}={10}^{x-7}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2x} & ={10}^{x-7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({10}^{x-7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)=(x-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =(x-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) & =-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x & =\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdb6db08-ce2e-5e89-ac1b-30cfe9f6e53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251295} {:answer/id #uuid "926b8b59-0c00-560e-8af1-404e27725e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251296} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd199d3-ff22-54b7-a8f3-88c6742eee51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251297} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1a0989-c177-578b-8f9a-c1dca813a14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251298} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e310b-f791-5855-9987-ec6a564dca1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccd199d3-ff22-54b7-a8f3-88c6742eee51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251297} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c97278-8fd5-505c-8034-ecd7054e4203" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251294}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3b45ed9-d96c-5211-9aac-db609bef71c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-242923" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${3}^{2x}={8}^{1-6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2x} & ={8}^{1-6x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{1-6x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a780f4a0-8450-5cb8-805a-2dd97752953c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\log(2)+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251302} {:answer/id #uuid "3dda0191-cfab-52a8-984d-da80f345ebf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\log(2)-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dda0191-cfab-52a8-984d-da80f345ebf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\log(2)-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251303} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d20689e-f160-5519-ba1e-e161d006fcd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251301}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a80e13c8-4b2d-570c-89d6-a9e588816f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-242938" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x+1}={2}^{x}?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x+1} & ={2}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1=\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1=x\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-x & =1 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-1) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "931a4ce6-7435-585c-9775-66d58253118b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251306} {:answer/id #uuid "50ba0cd1-2629-5afe-ab11-6821ac544fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251307} {:answer/id #uuid "42f06250-b7c9-580a-9166-2cfbdac14d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251308} {:answer/id #uuid "60d37d82-ee94-569c-aaf8-e1423aeb7f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251309} {:answer/id #uuid "a6df1250-c2da-5c4f-8078-44a8901d5507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6df1250-c2da-5c4f-8078-44a8901d5507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251310} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d093e8-d228-59bf-800a-810003a75896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251305}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed7ab01a-8bb8-585b-9681-7e499c6dc236" :question/math-academy-id "q-26385" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x-3}={7}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x-3} & ={7}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(7) & =3 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)) & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "604c19ce-2cf7-5112-b564-01e34a77f6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.544" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251313} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2c3f1f-ed08-5d4d-8b06-08b1901eb0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251314} {:answer/id #uuid "3170abaf-55a7-547a-a014-f7d9e1d3ff68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.293" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251315} {:answer/id #uuid "93b958a5-470b-501f-b39c-35b5c913a9af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.891" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251316} {:answer/id #uuid "c30da09b-1dc2-51f6-ae45-e0ba1424e165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.712" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b2c3f1f-ed08-5d4d-8b06-08b1901eb0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251314} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de15606-7cd8-52e0-9434-b6f499803b01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1b384d1-bf1d-5185-800b-b0cf5436aeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-26828" :question/problem "Given that ${5}^{y}={4}^{y+1},$ what is the value of $y?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{y}={4}^{y+1} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{y}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{5}⁡({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y & =y{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y-y{log}_{5}⁡4 & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y(1-{log}_{5}⁡4) & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡4}{1-{log}_{5}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡(4)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.861\\,353.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, we can approximate $y$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y≈\\frac{0.861\\,353}{1-0.861\\,353}≈6.213,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "281ac056-8d00-568b-897e-3a824bf12ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251320} {:answer/id #uuid "bddf0bc8-6186-5921-aa31-f0376dcf91cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251321} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae3b9a2-69ab-5139-8a18-2c9b64fc5911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251322} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dc6907-0543-5f80-b778-98317f9bc61e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251323} {:answer/id #uuid "c755523e-15e7-582b-8b1d-e426d69c1602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ae3b9a2-69ab-5139-8a18-2c9b64fc5911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251322} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a422cf4-3bc3-590f-a7e8-750f4534bb3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3907caa8-855e-5e3e-bfcf-8820788b0b2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66899" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{y+1}={3}^{2y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y+1} & ={3}^{2y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\ny+1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2y})\\,\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\ny+1=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-y & =1 \\\\ y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1) & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedabfab-10b5-5a7e-a720-848509e40d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{1-{log}_{4}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251327} {:answer/id #uuid "1557fbd5-7676-5204-bae1-81e06ddbb3ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(7)}{1-{log}_{4}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251328} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9bb326-f7f5-5924-a767-030e3cb258ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(7)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251329} {:answer/id #uuid "6f84c395-0374-505c-8c23-b3925fc3bc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{log}_{4}⁡(7)}{{log}_{4}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251330} {:answer/id #uuid "fab6d141-80ae-52c9-bcf9-20e364ae4d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{log}_{4}⁡(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cedabfab-10b5-5a7e-a720-848509e40d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{1-{log}_{4}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251327} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa5b5d6-2b8d-51d3-ae28-8f1b3e80b5b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04248f58-d370-5624-b1ab-718c32069b86" :question/math-academy-id "q-79359" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of ${log}_{4}$, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${4}^{x+1}={7}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{4}$ in both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x+1} & ={7}^{x} \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡({4}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{4}⁡({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{4}⁡({4}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{4}⁡({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{4}⁡({7}^{x}) \\\\ x+1 & =x{log}_{4}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{4}⁡(7) \\\\ x-x{log}_{4}⁡(7) & =-1 \\\\ x(1-{log}_{4}⁡(7)) & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{1-{log}_{4}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ba7bed8-2fcb-5ce8-a573-418cb622587b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+3}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251334} {:answer/id #uuid "8e051a48-9f3e-5519-b921-af8a747490be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251335} {:answer/id #uuid "4142750c-fb70-57d9-8eec-97746c2be1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251336} {:answer/id #uuid "59914179-1f1f-5ae7-bc87-8c68a64e8273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-1}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251337} {:answer/id #uuid "e496457e-5e24-5720-8c16-e71ba3af5128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ba7bed8-2fcb-5ce8-a573-418cb622587b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+3}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251334} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1a6d791-296e-51ac-b9cb-9eb5241ec1d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6265ed67-1024-5e56-ab7c-a131aba9f90b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79361" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-3}={5}^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & ={5}^{x+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx-3=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+1})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-3=(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x-3 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+3 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+3}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
